WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.810 --> 00:00:07.570
- Okay, so today what I
want to talk to you about

00:00:07.570 --> 00:00:11.310
a little bit is actually
how to drug design.

00:00:11.310 --> 00:00:13.230
I feel like this is a very relevant topic,

00:00:13.230 --> 00:00:15.290
especially with corona

00:00:15.290 --> 00:00:17.600
where, you know,
everybody's talking about,

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:18.910
well, they're talking about vaccines,

00:00:18.910 --> 00:00:21.650
but initially they were talking
about what drugs can we use

00:00:21.650 --> 00:00:23.080
for people who actually have corona?

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:25.050
You know, how can you alleviate symptoms

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:26.200
when people are coughing,

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:28.590
and need some- you know,
need to reduce inflammation

00:00:28.590 --> 00:00:32.090
in your body and their
response to the virus.

00:00:32.090 --> 00:00:34.010
So how can we design drugs for that?

00:00:34.010 --> 00:00:38.510
And I'll just take a very
simple drug that everybody knows

00:00:38.510 --> 00:00:39.343
or most people know.

00:00:39.343 --> 00:00:40.176
Advil, right?

00:00:40.176 --> 00:00:43.880
You have a headache, you
have, you know, muscle pain.

00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:45.050
Most people take Advil.

00:00:45.050 --> 00:00:47.144
And that relieves the symptoms.

00:00:47.144 --> 00:00:49.780
So Advil is, is the brand name.

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:53.240
Ibuprofen is the actual,
you know, drug name

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:56.640
and that has a certain
chemical structure to it.

00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:59.430
And the way they design sort of this,

00:00:59.430 --> 00:01:00.870
this molecule to look like this

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:03.680
is that they know exactly how to target.

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:05.780
They know how to
basically design this drug

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:10.420
because they know what the
actual target is of this drug.

00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:13.710
So in other words, there's, you know,

00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:18.710
some protein or enzyme
that you want to target.

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:22.580
And if you know the structure
of this enzyme or protein

00:01:22.580 --> 00:01:25.050
then you can design a drug
that can actually target

00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:26.320
that enzyme or protein.

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:29.820
And you may have heard this in AP Bio,

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:32.960
a sort of a model that you
use to describe an enzyme

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:34.610
and some sort of substrate binding to it

00:01:34.610 --> 00:01:36.890
is the lock and key model.

00:01:36.890 --> 00:01:40.205
And that's, again, you
have some sort of lock

00:01:40.205 --> 00:01:43.700
and you're designing a
key that can fit in it.

00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:47.360
But again, how do you
design this effective drug?

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:49.970
You have to know exactly
what this lock looks like.

00:01:49.970 --> 00:01:52.460
I just want to show you a
little bit of a cool video

00:01:52.460 --> 00:01:56.670
and I'll skip around a little
bit just for time purposes.

00:01:56.670 --> 00:01:59.551
But there's a company called Schrodinger

00:01:59.551 --> 00:02:03.010
that has cool tools

00:02:03.010 --> 00:02:04.513
basically to design,

00:02:06.970 --> 00:02:09.060
to have basically computational tools

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:11.070
to design certain drugs.

00:02:11.070 --> 00:02:12.410
And so I just want to show you this video,

00:02:12.410 --> 00:02:14.726
give you a little bit of a
background on what I mean.

00:02:14.726 --> 00:02:16.370
(upbeat music playing in video)

00:02:16.370 --> 00:02:19.503
Can you hear, by the way? The- okay.

00:02:36.377 --> 00:02:37.810
And so here, what they're showing you,

00:02:37.810 --> 00:02:39.970
I'll make it a little lower.

00:02:39.970 --> 00:02:42.450
They're showing you a protein and a drug

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:44.970
and they're showing you how
the drug goes into this protein

00:02:44.970 --> 00:02:46.523
and binds into this protein.

00:02:57.160 --> 00:02:59.620
And the proteins are made
up of these amino acids,

00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:01.710
they're basically a chain
of all these amino acids,

00:03:01.710 --> 00:03:04.260
which again are just carbon molecules

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:05.683
and other molecules as well.

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:09.560
And they can fold into
this particular shape.

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:12.210
And so they have this
three-dimensional structure

00:03:12.210 --> 00:03:13.600
which is really important to know

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:15.300
because when you think about proteins

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:19.600
they're not just, you know,
it's not just a flat 2D thing.

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:22.360
A lock is not just a flat 2D thing, right?

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:24.143
It's a three-dimensional structure.

00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:31.880
And this protein has
different elements to it.

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:34.990
So now it's just depicting the protein

00:03:34.990 --> 00:03:35.920
a little bit of a different way.

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:38.120
I'm going to skip through
this a little bit.

00:03:39.030 --> 00:03:41.640
But the most important thing
is that you have this drug

00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:44.948
and this drug can make different
contacts with the protein.

00:03:44.948 --> 00:03:46.070
It's going to show you now...

00:03:47.230 --> 00:03:49.070
Skip around a little bit.

00:03:49.070 --> 00:03:52.933
What type of contacts this drug
can make with this protein.

00:03:54.370 --> 00:03:56.090
So you can have hydrogen bonds

00:03:56.090 --> 00:03:57.410
of this drug to the different,

00:03:57.410 --> 00:03:59.100
you know, to the, to the protein.

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:00.080
You can have...

00:04:02.610 --> 00:04:07.430
It's going to show you hydrophobic bonds

00:04:07.430 --> 00:04:08.480
and, and so forth.

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:11.310
So I'm gonna stop this for right now

00:04:11.310 --> 00:04:13.290
because, just for the lack of time,

00:04:13.290 --> 00:04:15.633
but the idea is what this is showing you-

00:04:17.030 --> 00:04:18.520
one second-

00:04:18.520 --> 00:04:20.123
is that you have these,

00:04:22.208 --> 00:04:23.041
you have these,

00:04:24.170 --> 00:04:26.370
these drugs that make
certain contact points

00:04:27.744 --> 00:04:29.440
and chemical, you know,
chemical interactions

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:30.273
with the protein.

00:04:30.273 --> 00:04:33.270
And so if you find exactly
what this protein looks like

00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:36.070
you can design the drug
to make these contacts.

00:04:36.070 --> 00:04:39.520
And so the type of technique that we use

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:40.810
in our lab to actually find

00:04:40.810 --> 00:04:42.840
these three-dimensional structures

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:45.220
is called cryo-electron microscopy.

00:04:45.220 --> 00:04:48.810
So cryo means cold, right?

00:04:48.810 --> 00:04:52.610
So very cold, extreme cold temperatures.

00:04:52.610 --> 00:04:54.900
And then you have electro-microscopy

00:04:54.900 --> 00:04:57.450
because you actually use this microscope

00:04:57.450 --> 00:04:59.820
that shoots electrons into the sample

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:01.710
to visualize that sample.

00:05:01.710 --> 00:05:04.721
So I'm just going to
very, very briefly go at-

00:05:04.721 --> 00:05:07.040
go through this because I'm
going to show you this soon,

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:09.930
is that usually to get that
three-dimensional structure

00:05:09.930 --> 00:05:10.763
of your protein

00:05:10.763 --> 00:05:13.500
what you do is you have
your protein solution

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:16.040
and you place it on this small little grid

00:05:16.040 --> 00:05:18.080
which is basically the smaller- small-

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:20.883
like smaller than the
tip of your fingertip.

00:05:21.740 --> 00:05:24.060
And then you freeze
this and that's, again,

00:05:24.060 --> 00:05:25.870
the idea of the cryo condition.

00:05:25.870 --> 00:05:28.130
You need to freeze it so that

00:05:28.130 --> 00:05:33.130
you get it in one conformation,
in one static structure.

00:05:33.270 --> 00:05:35.950
So that way you can image
that static structure

00:05:35.950 --> 00:05:37.680
and come up with one conformation

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:39.050
of what that protein looks like

00:05:39.050 --> 00:05:42.600
and get a three-dimensional
structure of what that is.

