00:11:18.320 --> 00:11:20.260
for a few reasons,

00:11:20.260 --> 00:11:21.770
but the main one is you want to sort of

00:11:21.770 --> 00:11:24.540
get a static picture of your protein.

00:11:24.540 --> 00:11:26.190
You can't just put it in solution.

00:11:26.190 --> 00:11:27.310
You need a static picture.

00:11:27.310 --> 00:11:30.980
It's too dynamic, basically to be able to

00:11:32.810 --> 00:11:35.160
capture a picture and reconstruct

00:11:35.160 --> 00:11:37.730
a three-dimensional structure of it.

00:11:37.730 --> 00:11:40.840
So imagine if you had a protein
that's constantly moving.

00:11:40.840 --> 00:11:42.940
It's almost like saying
a blurry picture, right?

00:11:42.940 --> 00:11:43.970
If somebody is running

00:11:43.970 --> 00:11:45.700
it's really hard to
capture what they look like

00:11:45.700 --> 00:11:48.600
because you have a blurry picture, right.

00:11:48.600 --> 00:11:51.680
But if you have somebody
just standing in place

00:11:51.680 --> 00:11:54.470
then what happens is you
can capture that, you know

00:11:54.470 --> 00:11:56.630
they're- what they look like

00:11:56.630 --> 00:11:58.942
if they're just standing in place.

00:11:58.942 --> 00:12:01.840
So, and in addition to that

00:12:01.840 --> 00:12:03.050
there's some other technicalities

00:12:03.050 --> 00:12:05.120
of having a certain ice thickness

00:12:05.120 --> 00:12:08.030
so that the electrons can
go through it as well.

00:12:08.030 --> 00:12:09.634
So you need to a certain type of ice

00:12:09.634 --> 00:12:14.203
for the electrons to
sort of permeate through.

