00:13:16.800 --> 00:13:19.270
- [Student] So if you aren't originally,

00:13:19.270 --> 00:13:22.180
like you're taking the
sample in the solution

00:13:22.180 --> 00:13:25.330
and then you're free-
you're like freezing it.

00:13:25.330 --> 00:13:28.074
Why would it denature when you're-

00:13:28.074 --> 00:13:30.000
when taken out of the liquid nitrogen

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:32.370
if it wasn't denaturing, like, before?

00:13:32.370 --> 00:13:34.950
- [Ayala] Yeah. Well. yeah. (unclear)

00:13:34.950 --> 00:13:36.750
So there's always a risk basically

00:13:36.750 --> 00:13:40.230
of freeze-thawing your protein

00:13:40.230 --> 00:13:41.400
because what happens is

00:13:42.780 --> 00:13:46.170
you basically, you have it in this,

00:13:46.170 --> 00:13:49.720
this- basically first you
have it in a solution, right?

00:13:49.720 --> 00:13:50.680
And then you freeze it.

00:13:50.680 --> 00:13:52.310
But if you freeze it, it's possible

00:13:52.310 --> 00:13:54.407
there's ice crystals that form,

00:13:54.407 --> 00:13:56.780
it's possible that when you, you know,

00:13:56.780 --> 00:13:59.140
it's at a certain
temperature, there may be,

00:13:59.140 --> 00:14:01.430
there's aggregates of the protein.

00:14:01.430 --> 00:14:03.660
And so when you unfreeze
it, there's always a risk

00:14:03.660 --> 00:14:06.360
of basically having some
protein that goes bad.

00:14:06.360 --> 00:14:09.871
And so actually, anytime you,
you let's say freeze a protein

00:14:09.871 --> 00:14:12.330
in -80 degrees or something

00:14:12.330 --> 00:14:14.610
you always have to spin it down again.

00:14:14.610 --> 00:14:17.330
So that way the good protein
stays in your solution

00:14:17.330 --> 00:14:19.610
but the bad protein
that basically denatured

00:14:19.610 --> 00:14:21.373
goes to the bottom, in pellets.

00:14:23.533 --> 00:14:25.440
And so just on a technical level

00:14:25.440 --> 00:14:26.670
you have that little grid, right?

00:14:26.670 --> 00:14:29.610
You basically have three
microliters of a droplet on there.

00:14:29.610 --> 00:14:31.840
If you take it out and thaw it out,

00:14:31.840 --> 00:14:33.180
you would hypothetically,

00:14:33.180 --> 00:14:35.180
you need to just take
those three microliters

00:14:35.180 --> 00:14:37.049
and then spin it down again,

00:14:37.049 --> 00:14:38.060
so you wouldn't have all this protein,

00:14:38.060 --> 00:14:40.683
half the protein denatured
in your solution.

00:14:42.687 --> 00:14:43.610
So it's technically,

00:14:43.610 --> 00:14:47.150
you can't technically do that
once it's already on the grid.

00:14:47.150 --> 00:14:48.692
But yeah, the short answer is that

00:14:48.692 --> 00:14:51.490
you always have that risk
whenever you freeze-thaw

00:14:51.490 --> 00:14:53.800
because of ice crystal formation.

00:14:53.800 --> 00:14:56.630
And because of either aggregates

00:14:56.630 --> 00:14:58.850
in the solution that when frozen

00:14:58.850 --> 00:15:00.973
they basically just fall apart.

00:15:02.620 --> 00:15:05.780
I don't know, that- does
that make sense a little bit?

00:15:05.780 --> 00:15:06.630
- [Student] Yeah.

00:15:06.630 --> 00:15:07.463
- [Ayala] Okay.

00:15:09.010 --> 00:15:10.885
- [Teacher] So why will- I guess also

00:15:10.885 --> 00:15:12.859
Why won't they freeze?

00:15:12.859 --> 00:15:14.960
Why are they good if you're freezing them?

00:15:14.960 --> 00:15:16.800
Why don't they denature
when you freeze them?

00:15:16.800 --> 00:15:20.360
- [Ayala] Yeah. So sometimes
you do have that issue,

00:15:20.360 --> 00:15:22.750
but a lot of times what you will do

00:15:22.750 --> 00:15:24.910
is incorporate certain things inside.

00:15:24.910 --> 00:15:27.200
Like, you know, when
let's say you freeze cells

00:15:27.200 --> 00:15:30.440
you incorporate like glycerols
or something like sugars

00:15:30.440 --> 00:15:34.540
or you put, sometimes you
put in these detergents

00:15:34.540 --> 00:15:35.810
if there is that risk.

00:15:35.810 --> 00:15:38.220
So every sample is a little bit different

00:15:38.220 --> 00:15:41.620
but when you freeze it, when
the actual freezing happens

00:15:41.620 --> 00:15:45.160
it's so quick in the liquid
ethane that it doesn't denature

00:15:45.160 --> 00:15:47.110
but there are some proteins that don't,

00:15:48.046 --> 00:15:49.670
that don't retain their
structure like that.

00:15:49.670 --> 00:15:53.651
And so that, that's, you know, sometimes

00:15:53.651 --> 00:15:55.843
and you have to optimize your conditions,

00:15:55.843 --> 00:15:59.630
like again putting in detergents or sugars

00:15:59.630 --> 00:16:02.083
that will help stabilize
it during that process.

