00:16:18.920 --> 00:16:21.032
Cause like, you were holding the grid

00:16:21.032 --> 00:16:22.810
at different angles and
it just was staying on it.

00:16:22.810 --> 00:16:25.140
- [Ayala] Yeah. Well, so I
didn't get to show you this

00:16:25.140 --> 00:16:27.620
but normally what we do
actually in the machine,

00:16:27.620 --> 00:16:29.373
it's a really cool machine.

00:16:29.373 --> 00:16:31.707
I'll- I'll send you guys
a video to check out

00:16:31.707 --> 00:16:35.350
but what you normally do
is actually blot your grid.

00:16:35.350 --> 00:16:39.480
So you basically take, and
maybe I should show this before,

00:16:39.480 --> 00:16:42.700
but you basically take like a paper

00:16:42.700 --> 00:16:44.780
like a filter paper and you blot it.

00:16:44.780 --> 00:16:46.620
So you have the grid here and
then you have the filter paper

00:16:46.620 --> 00:16:48.430
and it slams down like that.

00:16:48.430 --> 00:16:50.500
And it absorbs the liquid.

00:16:50.500 --> 00:16:54.820
And as it absorbs, what
happens is the protein sticks

00:16:54.820 --> 00:16:56.490
to the, on the other side, right?

00:16:56.490 --> 00:16:59.920
So you have the droplet here,
you have the grid over here

00:16:59.920 --> 00:17:01.850
and then you have the filter paper.

00:17:01.850 --> 00:17:05.000
And as the liquid goes through the grid

00:17:06.090 --> 00:17:08.060
into the filter paper, it's absorbed.

00:17:08.060 --> 00:17:10.190
And then you have the
protein that sort of sticks

00:17:10.190 --> 00:17:12.900
to the side of the grid and
just kind of stays there.

00:17:12.900 --> 00:17:15.383
And then immediately after it gets frozen.

00:17:16.810 --> 00:17:18.080
So you have that

00:17:18.080 --> 00:17:19.960
because you have that
step, it helps it stick.

00:17:19.960 --> 00:17:23.270
And in addition to that, the car- the grid

00:17:23.270 --> 00:17:25.240
that little small thing
actually has a piece

00:17:25.240 --> 00:17:26.110
of carbon

00:17:27.712 --> 00:17:29.145
and basically

00:17:29.145 --> 00:17:31.150
carbon sticks to a lot of things.

00:17:31.150 --> 00:17:34.350
It sticks to, it will always
have things stick to it.

00:17:34.350 --> 00:17:37.383
So it helps sort of
bring the protein there.

00:17:39.202 --> 00:17:41.913
But so there's an extra step, essentially.

00:17:43.529 --> 00:17:45.770
I'm just saying, I, I didn't know anything

00:17:45.770 --> 00:17:47.330
about this before I
came to graduate school

00:17:47.330 --> 00:17:50.130
